See [asm_analysis.md](Ressources/asm_analysis.md) for the complete ASM analysis.

After the ASM analysis, we can see that for the program to run system("/bin/sh") we need g_ptr1[32] to not be equal to zero. and the buffer to start with "login"

This pointer is allocated on the heap when the given buffer starts with "auth "

Only 4 bytes are allocated, so we can't write directly on g_ptr1[32].

However, there is another pointer g_ptr2 that is allocated with strdup when the buffer starts with "service" 

level8@RainFall:~$ ./level8 
(nil), (nil) 
auth 
0x804a008, (nil) 
service
0x804a008, 0x804a018 

We can see that g_ptr2 is allocated at an address 16 bytes after g_ptr1:

g_ptr1 = 0x804a008
g_ptr2 = 0x804a018

Therefore:

g_ptr2 - g_ptr1 = 0x10 = 16

The condition checked by the program is located at offset 0x20 (32 bytes) from g_ptr1.

Since g_ptr2 starts 16 bytes after g_ptr1, this location is 16 bytes after the beginning of the memory pointed to by g_ptr2.

Because g_ptr2 contains a string copied with strdup, sufficiently long input can make the copied string extend into this region. This can cause the value read at g_ptr1 + 32 to be non-zero, satisfying the condition required to reach the shell.

level8@RainFall:~$ ./level8 
(nil), (nil) 
auth 
0x804a008, (nil) 
service0123456789abcdef
0x804a008, 0x804a018 
login
$ cat /home/user/level9/.pass
